kundor <kundor@gmail.com> wrote:
> Well, if drow are evil elves, it seems that ~drow would be good orcs.

I don't think I agree. Ruining something doesn't always mean making
it the opposite of what it started out. If I let a bomb off in
someone's bedroom and their bed was tidy, it becomes a mess. If I do
it when their bed was messy, it _stays_ a mess.

NetHack conflates order with good and chaos with evil to some extent
(having only three alignments rather than nine), so I think
`ruining' an evil (chaotic) creature ought under no circumstances to
make it go good (lawful).

> They would live on the surface and only go out in the daytime, and hate
> their normal, evil orc brethren.

I don't agree with this either, in fact. `Orcs hate everyone, even
other orcs'. So if you _did_ invert an orc, it wouldn't hate its
brethren. It would _love_ everything, even other (inverted and
normal) orcs :-)
